  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDespite reasonable model consistency regarding synoptic\nfeatures, considerable forecast uncertainty remains in the\nmesoscale regarding convective evolution. The polar jet remains\nlargely north of the region through the period, with an\nexpansive upper ridge centered over the southern United States.\nWeak mid-level disturbances rotating around the southern periphery\nof this ridge will provide sufficient forcing for ascent to \ninitiate convection, which may subsequently grow upscale into \nthunderstorm complexes. These complexes will propagate \nsoutheastward down the instability gradient driven by persistent\nheat and humidity from the plains. Overall confidence in the\ntrack and timing of any of these features is low.\n\nThe thermodynamic environment is characterized by precipitable\nwater values near the top of climatological distribution for\nmid-August, ranging from 2.0 to approaching 2.5 inches with deep\nwarm cloud depths supporting highly efficient rainfall\nproduction. In the absence of being overturned upstream, MUCAPE\nvalues will range from 2000-4000J/kg at times with persistent\nregeneration of decent mid-level lapse rates owing to the\naforementioned heat extending back into the High Plains. \n\nOverall, stronger deep layer shear associated with the belt of\nstronger flow over the ridge will remain separated from the\nstronger corridor of instability south of the ridge with\nconcerns for severe thunderstorms largely limited to upscale\ngrowth or isolated wind damage associated with microbursts.\n\nThere is at least some potential advertised by some WRF core\nmembers in a nose of a stronger H850 low-level jet near 30 to \n35KT crossing the Middle Ohio Valley in association with an \nMCV Friday morning into Friday afternoon. This would yield at\nleast a localized higher potential for severe storms however, \nthis feature remains an outlier among the guidance suite, \nhighlighting the overall low confidence levels.\n\nDespite some amplification of the ridge displacing stronger \nflow even farther north toward the latter half of the weekend \nwhich would typically shelter us from summer time ridge riders \nso still see some potential for a continuation of this pattern \ngiven the expected strong instability gradient under the ridge.\n\nIn 25 years in the weather business the preceding and forecast\nactivity associated with this pattern represents some of the\nlowest forecast confidence levels in the details (despite fair\nagreement in synoptic features) I have ever experienced.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe decision regarding the ongoing Flash Flood Watch remains\nchallenging from a messaging perspective. Showers and isolated\nstorms may impact western counties and the Metro Valley this\nmorning, but additional organized heavy rainfall is expected to\nhold off until this evening into Friday morning. Additional\nheavy rain remains possible through Friday, potentially \nextending through the weekend, but forecast confidence is very \npoor due to model struggles with convective advection along the\nring of fire in weaker steering flow south of the ridge crest. \n\nLush summer vegetation has assisted in soil recovery following\nearlier rainfall this week, allowing many basins to recover\nsomewhat, now able to handle 1 to 1.5 inches of rain in a short\nduration. In a higher confidence scenario, canceling the \ncurrent watch (likely concurrent with several neighboring\noffices planning to expire their watches on time this morning) \nand reissuing later with a deferred start time would be \npreferred for messaging purposes. However, given substantial \nuncertainty over even the next 24 hours, confidence is \ninsufficient to alter the existing watch at this time. As a \nresult, the watch continues as issued for now through Friday \nmorning for potential localized high water issues, with an \neventual extension possible if confidence increases in the \naforementioned potential scenarios.",
